`_lines = StreamController<String>()` — NOT broadcast, and **WITHOUT a listener, events added are BUFFERED** (StreamController buffers until listened). So `sendLine('go')` before the REPL listens is fine — the line is delivered when the listener attaches. Not the issue.

BUT — `sendLine` on a single-subscription controller AFTER it has a listener is also fine. OK so input delivery works.

So the state at timeout is genuinely unknown (eager interpolation). Let me make the reason LAZY — waitForIt takes a String; I'll instead print AFTER the timeout using a fresh read... can't — the test already failed. Change waitForIt calls: wrap with a manual timeout that reads io.out at fail time. Simplest: change the condition closure to CAPTURE the last observed state:

```dart
var snapshot = '';
await waitForIt(() {
  final ok = turnRequests(stream).length >= 2;
  if (!ok) snapshot = 'turns=${turnRequests(stream).length} calls=${stream.contexts.length} out=${io.out.toString().length}';
  return ok;
}, reason: '...'); 
```
But reason is still eager — put the snapshot into a variable printed via `fail` ourselves... Actually simplest: make my own wait loop in the test:

```dart
Future<void> waitUntil(bool Function() cond, String Function() describe) async {
  for (var i = 0; i < 5000; i++) {
    if (cond()) return;
    await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 5));
  }
  fail('timed out: ${describe()}');
}
```

Use `describe()` lazy. Add to the test file locally. Then rerun to see the REAL state.

