NOW I see it precisely. The settle notice (⚙ block) shows my label: 'Command: cat > heredoc-target.md << 'EOF' heredoc body line 1 padding the command heredoc body line 2 padding the command heredoc […2172 chars…] c body line 56 padding the command heredoc body line 57 padding the command heredoc body line 58 padding the command EOF'.

The assertion `isNot(contains('heredoc body line'))` — the literal substring 'heredoc body line' APPEARS (lines 1, 2, 56, 57, 58 — the head/tail!). I misread _bodyMarker as the assertion target — the test asserts the GENERIC 'heredoc body line' substring (line 34 defines the MARKER for line 17, but the EXPECT uses the generic 'heredoc body line'). ANY body line in the label violates it.

THE OWNER INVARIANT (issue #599 RED contract): 'the body is never a transcript row source — the preview is one line + …'. My flatten+tail BREAKS the spirit: the tail 120 chars of a flattened heredoc = 3 body lines.

THE FIX: the label must keep the ticket's bounding AND the owner's never-render-the-body rule. The tail's PURPOSE (per my doc): 'the tail names the actual action of a long pipeline (`... ; make test`)'. For a multi-line heredoc, the ACTION is the OPENER (first line); the tail picks up body noise. Resolution: take the tail from the LAST LINE ONLY — the last line of a multi-line command is the terminator/action line; for a one-line pipeline (the bench evidence shape) the last line IS the whole command → behavior unchanged for the ticket's evidence case. And if the last line is short/empty (a command ending in 'EOF'), still bounded.

Rule: tail source = the last line of the ORIGINAL (unflattened) command; head = flattened head. Hmm — but then the label mixes flattened head + raw tail... Let me define cleanly:

```dart
String shellJobCommandLabel(String command, {head, tail}) {
  final flat = flatten(command);
  final headPart = slice(flat, 0, head);
  if (fits) return flat;
  // The tail cut sources from the command's LAST LINE only (issue #599):
  // a heredoc body never re-enters the transcript through the tail —
  // body lines sit in the omitted middle by construction.
  final lastLine = flat.substring(flat.lastIndexOf('\n') + 1); // flat has no \n!
```

Wait — flat has no newlines (whitespace flattened). Source the last line from the ORIGINAL command: `command.trim()`'s last line → flatten THAT line → tail cut. Then omitted count = flat.length − head − tailPart (honest total omitted).

Edge cases:
- One-line command: last line == whole command (trimmed) → tail = its last 120 — matches current behavior (bench pipelines keep '; make test' ✓).
- Multi-line heredoc: head = opener..., tail = 'EOF' (last line) — body lines NEVER appear ✓.
- Multi-line pipeline where the last line is the action: ✓.

The omitted count stays TRUE (flat length minus both parts). The marker '…N chars…' unchanged.

Update the doc comment to name the #599 invariant. Update my label test: add a case asserting a multi-line heredoc's label contains NO body lines (only opener head + last-line tail). The 60-job fixture: single-line pipelines — unchanged.

TDD: RED first — add the failing assertion to my label test (a heredoc-shaped command → label must not contain 'body line 17'), watch it fail, then fix, then re-run: label test + the PTY test + the 60-job fixture.

